  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert $A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$ into $\\frac{A}{2j}e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)} - \\frac{A}{2j}e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}$ by interpreting sine as the imaginary-axis projection of a rotating phasor.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A sine wave is the imaginary-axis projection of a rotating phasor, so the algebraic form we want is the one that exposes that projection directly. Start from Euler's formulas and subtract the conjugate pair; that is the shortest path from phasor motion to a sine identity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\ne^{j\\theta} &= \\cos\\theta + j\\sin\\theta \\\\\\ne^{-j\\theta} &= \\cos\\theta - j\\sin\\theta\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Subtract the second formula from the first. The cosine terms cancel, and the sine terms combine into a factor of $2j$, which is the algebraic sign that the imaginary-axis projection is being isolated.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\theta} - e^{-j\\theta} = 2j\\sin\\theta",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now solve for $\\sin\\theta$ to get the base identity used throughout this topic. This is the unit, zero-phase case, and it is the form to keep in mind before amplitude and phase are added.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} - e^{-j\\theta}}{2j}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now replace $\\theta$ with $\\omega t + \\phi$. The same identity becomes the sine of a time-varying phase, and multiplying by $A$ gives the general amplitude-phase form that later examples will reuse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi) = \\frac{A}{2j}e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)} - \\frac{A}{2j}e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the standard rewrite is one sine wave represented as a matched positive-frequency and negative-frequency pair. Later examples keep the same structure and only change the amplitude or phase, not the underlying exponential difference.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Converting a Unit-Amplitude Sine with Zero Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $\\sin(\\omega t)$ into a difference of complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit-amplitude, zero-phase sine $\\sin(\\omega t)$, so we use the base identity from the tutorial step. Because the amplitude is $1$ and the phase is $0$, no extra factors are needed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} - e^{-j\\theta}}{2j}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $\\theta=\\omega t$. The conjugate pair stays intact, and the only change is the time-dependent phase inside each exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin(\\omega t) &= \\frac{e^{j\\omega t} - e^{-j\\omega t}}{2j} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2j}e^{j\\omega t} - \\frac{1}{2j}e^{-j\\omega t}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the zero-phase, unit-amplitude rewrite of a sine wave as a difference of positive-frequency and negative-frequency exponentials. Later example steps will keep this same structure and only add amplitude or phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Including Amplitude in the Sine Exponential Difference",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $A\\sin(\\omega t)$ into a difference of complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same zero-phase sine from the previous example, but now with a scalar amplitude $A$ in front. That means we keep the same exponential-difference structure and carry the factor $A$ through the entire identity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin(\\omega t) = \\frac{e^{j\\omega t} - e^{-j\\omega t}}{2j}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply both sides by $A$. The amplitude does not change the positive-frequency and negative-frequency pair; it only scales both terms by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nA\\sin(\\omega t) &= A\\cdot\\frac{e^{j\\omega t} - e^{-j\\omega t}}{2j} \\\\\n&= \\frac{A}{2j}e^{j\\omega t} - \\frac{A}{2j}e^{-j\\omega t}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the amplitude-scaled sine is the same conjugate exponential difference, with $A$ attached to both sides of the pair. The structure is unchanged; only the overall scale is different.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Including Phase in the Sine Exponential Difference",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$ into a difference of complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phase shift inside the sine argument. Treat the whole argument as $\\theta=\\omega t+\\phi$ and reuse the same sine identity from the previous examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin\\theta=\\frac{e^{j\\theta}-e^{-j\\theta}}{2j}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $\\theta=\\omega t+\\phi$ into both exponentials. The phase stays inside the argument of each term, so the positive-frequency and negative-frequency parts shift together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}-e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}}{2j}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phase-shifted sine has the same exponential-difference structure as before, with the same argument in both terms. The only change from the zero-phase case is that $\\phi$ rides inside each exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Sines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Converting a General Amplitude-Phase Sine",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)$ into a difference of complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is both the amplitude $A$ and the phase shift $\\phi$. Start from the phase-only identity and then scale the entire expression by $A$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}-e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}}{2j}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiplying by $A$ carries the same factor through both exponentials. The phase stays inside both arguments, and the amplitude scales both terms equally.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A\\sin(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\frac{A}{2j}e^{j(\\omega t+\\phi)}-\\frac{A}{2j}e^{-j(\\omega t+\\phi)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the full exponential-difference form for a sine with amplitude and phase. The positive-frequency and negative-frequency terms share the same phase argument; only the overall scale changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
